xwayland: a pointer motion no mouse made comes with a zero relative motion

XWayland posts a wl_pointer.motion whose frame has no zwp_relative_pointer_v1
motion on an absolute pointer device of its own (xwayland-pointer, Abs X and
Abs Y), and every other motion on its relative pointer device
(xwayland-relative-pointer, Rel X and Rel Y). The X server switches the core
pointer's valuators to those of the device that sent last, so each such
motion turns the core pointer's axes absolute until the mouse moves again.
Hyprland sends motions without a relative one when no mouse moved: a window
mapped, tiled or fullscreened under the pointer, a warp, a constraint's
cursor hint, and an absolute device's motion.

SDL before 3.4.16 reads the core pointer's axes once, at the first raw motion
it sees, and keeps them for the life of the game (3.4.16 follows
XI_DeviceChanged). A game whose window is placed under a still pointer reads
the absolute device's axes, and from then on takes each relative raw motion
for a position: the first motion of the mouse jumps by the pointer's
position, each later one moves by the difference between two motions, and a
turned view twitches and comes back. Minecraft 26.3 ships SDL 3.4.14 (LWJGL
3.4.3); its camera would not turn.

Hyprland now sends XWayland a zero relative motion with a pointer motion that
had none in its frame, so XWayland posts every motion on its relative device.
The position is the same, the raw motion is zero, and the core pointer's axes
stay relative. A mouse's motions are sent as before, and Wayland clients get
nothing new.

For Nova: packaging/hyprland/PKGBUILD applies this to Hyprland 0.57.2's
release tarball after the other three patches (patch -Np1); it touches none
of their files. packaging/hyprland/UPSTREAM.md is the pull request it is
offered as.

